       "summary": "**Evaluating character design**\n\nI'm noticing that there are extensive changes in regions like the helmet, eyes, and the brim/body. The pixel craft looks clean overall, though some clusters seem a bit awkward. The character appears to have a canonical sprite style. I wonder if the helmet in frame 2 shifted? Overall, I\u2019d rate motion readability a 6, consistency a 5, economy a 4, pixel craft an 8, and appeal a 6, giving it a total of 29 out of 50. It seems competent, but maybe not convincing enough at 58. There are specific issues in frame two.**Analyzing sprite design changes**\n\nI'm considering how extensive XOR due to 1px changes is still economical; a single pixel move affects outline edges but isn't costly for animation ROM. The pixel craft seems interesting, especially with the black void in the center and white eye bars. The body under the helmet changes dramatically, possibly due to eye positions. There may also be a difference in the helmet shading. It looks like the entire sprite might be mirrored, which could explain the walking alternates and highlights shifting. The helmet's shading and shape appear asymmetric, suggesting a mirroring technique was used.**Examining sprite mirroring techniques**\n\nI\u2019m noticing that mirroring sprites is a common, cost-effective trick in NES games. It can alternate direction but may cause issues with lighting effects and face orientation, which is problematic if the character is walking in the same direction. However, Capcom seems able to mirror leg tiles. Looking at the frames: the helmet's top highlight shifts from around 106 in frame 1 to around 226 in frame 2, translating to a 6-pixel left shift. The entire helmet appears to move to the left by one pixel source.",
